description:
## Error Boundaries

A JavaScript error in a child component shouldn't take down your whole app. **Error boundaries** are special components that catch errors during rendering and show a fallback UI instead.

There's no hook equivalent yet — boundaries must be **class components** that implement at least one of:

- `static getDerivedStateFromError(error)` — returns next state when a child throws
- `componentDidCatch(error, info)` — for logging

```jsx
class Boundary extends React.Component {
  state = { hasError: false };
  static getDerivedStateFromError() { return { hasError: true }; }
  render() {
    if (this.state.hasError) return <p>Something went wrong</p>;
    return this.props.children;
  }
}
```

### Your Task
Build an error boundary that protects a buggy button:
1. Define class `ErrorBoundary` with `hasError` state and `getDerivedStateFromError`
2. When caught, render `<p id="error-fallback">Something went wrong</p>`
3. Otherwise, render `this.props.children`
4. Wrap the provided `<BuggyButton />` in `<ErrorBoundary>`

src/App.jsx.contents:
import React, { useState, useEffect, useRef, useMemo, useCallback, useReducer, createContext, useContext, Component } from 'react';

// Build your ErrorBoundary class here

function BuggyButton() {
  const [crash, setCrash] = useState(false);
  if (crash) throw new Error('Boom!');
  return <button id="crash-btn" onClick={() => setCrash(true)}>Crash</button>;
}

function App() {
  return (
    <div>
      <BuggyButton />
    </div>
  );
}

export default App;

